NAME
    coverage - the coverage map: which test classes executed a source file

SYNOPSIS
    coverage [FILE...]
    coverage run --bin DIR [--tree DIR] [--jobs N] [--rev REV] [--class C]... [--by-case C]... [--nightly] [--out DIR] [--repo DIR]
    coverage load [--merge] [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    A command of the emusen.testing module. The map records, for each
    test class of a test project, the source files its tests executed:
    C# files of this repository by their path, and the LunaP toolkit's
    by the sibling checkout's path with a 'lunap:' prefix. impact's
    'covered' rule reads it: a changed .cs file the map has seen selects
    the classes that executed it and no more. See `man impact` and
    EmuSen_Testing.md §3.7.

    With no words, one line describes the map the store holds: the
    revision it was built at, when, how many classes and files, and how
    many classes gave no report. With FILE words it prints one line per
    class that executed each file, or 'unseen' for a file the map never
    saw (a new file, one no test executes, or any file that is not C#).

    'run' builds a map from one build of the test project. Every class
    of the land tier runs in its own `dotnet test` process under the
    coverlet collector (the version the test project references, from
    the NuGet cache) with the filter
    (FullyQualifiedName~CLASS.|FullyQualifiedName~CLASS+)&Category!=nightly,
    so the map shows what land runs; the nightly tier's classes are left
    out (--nightly adds them), since the nightly runs its classes whole
    and needs no map. --jobs N processes run at once, each from its own
    full copy of --bin beside it (bin/coverage-N/<framework>), never a
    hard link: coverlet rewrites the instrumented assemblies in place
    and restores them at the end of each session, and a build rewrites
    the rest, so nothing may be shared with the build or between
    workers. The copies are removed at the end. A class whose process
    ends without a report is run once more after five seconds, since
    the previous class's process may still hold a module coverlet backs
    up. --tree names
    the checkout the build was made in, whose paths the pdbs carry
    (default: the repository); LunaP's root is read from that tree's
    LunaP.props. --class C limits the run to the classes whose name
    holds C, for a smoke test. Each process runs in the project's capped
    scope with MSBUILDDISABLENODEREUSE=1 and UseSharedCompilation=false
    in its environment (VSTest takes no -p: for a dll), so a test that
    builds leaves no compiler server behind.

    xunit discovers every test before the filter applies, so each copy
    gets an xunit.runner.json with preEnumerateTheories false (a
    theory's data is then evaluated by the class that runs it, not by
    discovery), and the run opens with a discovery baseline: two runs
    whose filter matches no test, the least hit count of each line of
    each class block kept. A file counts as executed by a class only
    where a line's hits exceed that floor; a line discovery alone hits
    counts for nobody.

    --by-case C maps a class whose name holds C one group of cases at a
    time instead of whole: its cases are listed (dotnet test
    --list-tests, with theories enumerated at discovery for that copy)
    and grouped by the first quoted argument of each display name, less
    a trailing size such as -1280x800 (the view a fit audit draws, the
    recording it reads back), the cases with no quoted argument forming
    one more group. Each group is a unit of its own, CLASS#KEY (CLASS#
    for the last), selected and run by the filter
    (FullyQualifiedName~CLASS.&(DisplayName~"KEY"|DisplayName~"KEY-)),
    so a change to one window's code runs that window's cases and no
    other's. The groups' reports are read against a second baseline
    taken with theories enumerated at discovery, since that is how they
    run. The output is plain lines, one a line as classes finish:

        # coverage 1  rev R  built T  bin DIR  jobs N  classes C  groups G
        # baseline  N files  M lines  SECONDS
        covered  UNIT  FILE           the unit executed the file
        ran      UNIT  SECONDS  RESULT  N files
        none     UNIT  why            no report (its log is kept under --out)
        # done   N with a report, M without

    A class with no report (the process crashed before coverlet wrote
    it) is recorded under the file '*' and impact selects it for every
    covered change, so a lost report widens the selection and never
    narrows it. The status is 1 when any class gave no report.

    'load' reads a run's lines from FILE or standard input into the
    store, as one trial of tier 'coverage' with the run's revision as
    its tree, replacing the previous map in one transaction. With
    --merge it replaces only the rows of the classes the run holds (a
    class mapped by case merged into a whole map), and the run must be
    at the map's revision. impact
    uses the newest map whose revision is at most one day older than
    the change's merge base (its --map-age), else says so and falls
    back to the graph rule.

    Measured on 2026-11-02 on the desktop: a class costs about 10 s
    more under coverlet than bare (instrumenting the 17 EmuSen
    assemblies and writing a 34 MB report), so 526 land classes in 4
    processes take about an hour; a file executed by every class
    (xunit's discovery runs the test attributes' constructors) is
    attributed to every class, which is correct, since a change there
    reaches every run.

EXAMPLES
    coverage
    coverage EmuSen.Mistress/Views/CoresWindow.cs | wc -l
    coverage run --bin EmuSen.WiseMan/bin/Debug/net10.0 --jobs 4 > map.txt
    coverage load map.txt
    coverage run --bin EmuSen.WiseMan/bin/Debug/net10.0 --class GalaxiaScopeTests --jobs 1
